## Design Consistency & Limitations

- Its required to read the [Graphics Team Reference](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1s4X7T7eMOj9ZTimwnP_Ok1Bfs8yKI22T8qqddm7MTQ8/edit?usp=sharing) and the [Art Standards](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hFSdY6zVBxsenvYRairC2hBy67O43v8J6SDU-CjzxdM/edit?usp=sharing) to understand how graphics on the DS works, and the art standards/limitations that apply across the whole project. You need to follow this at all times!
- Always design with the Nintendo DS's screen proportions in mind, even for early concepts. It's better to know how something will look at that size than to find out later it doesn't work.
- Sprites and menus are limited to **16 colors** (including transparency), with F112EF reserved as the transparency key color. (Maybe with a couple of exceptions with large images where a transparency color isn't necessary, but it will be discussed in specific cases)
- Each individual sprite can have its own color palette. Use this to your advantage when building out the UI by mixing different sprites, but keep no more than 16 palettes active at the same time. You won't need that many, so don't overdo it. The less palettes used, the better.

---

## AI Usage Policy

There will be **NO** usage of Generative AI within the UX/UI Team. Anyone found to be using it to produce UX/UI for the project will be kicked from the team, and have their contributions removed.

---

## Tools & Setup

There isn’t one strict software that the team uses, but the primary file format must be .psd. This keeps layers, text, and edits portable across the whole team.

Software that the team uses include:

### [Adobe Photoshop](https://www.adobe.com/products/photoshop.html)

The industry standard, and the tool most of our references and existing files were built in. If you already have a license, this is the safest default.

### [Affinity](https://www.affinity.studio/)

A free, professional app alternative to Photoshop.

### [Photopea](https://www.photopea.com/)

A free, browser-based editor with a near-identical interface to Photoshop, including native .psd support. The go-to option if you don't have access to Photoshop.

### [GIMP](https://www.gimp.org/)

A free, open-source alternative. GIMP can import and export .psd files, though some advanced Photoshop-specific features (adjustment layers, smart objects, layer effects, etc.) may not translate perfectly — worth double-checking your file after export.

Whenever possible, stick to PSD-compatible tools so files can be shared and edited seamlessly across the team. Consistency here saves everyone headaches down the line.

